# Run [[ solution ]] on your machine

Generated by ExiQO Migrate from TIBCO BusinessWorks application `[[ app_id ]]`.
This folder is the whole migrated application: the .NET solution, its tests, the container image,
the Terraform and Terragrunt infrastructure, and the CI/CD pipelines. Nothing here needs ExiQO
Migrate to run.

## Quickest path: Docker

```bash
bash run-local.sh
```

On Windows:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File run-local.ps1
```

That builds the image from `Dockerfile`, starts Postgres and the API through
`docker-compose.override.yml`, waits for `GET /health`, then prints the Swagger address and a bearer
token to paste into Swagger's **Authorize** dialog.

* Swagger UI: <http://localhost:[[ port ]]/swagger>
* Health (no token): <http://localhost:[[ port ]]/health>

## Without Docker: the .NET [[ dotnet_version ]] SDK

Needs a Postgres on `localhost:5432` with user `app`, password `app`, and
[% if databases | length == 1 %]the database `[[ databases[0] ]]`[% else %]the databases [% for db in databases %]`[[ db ]]`[% if not loop.last %], [% endif %][% endfor %][% endif %].

```bash
bash run-local.sh --dotnet          # or: powershell -File run-local.ps1 -Dotnet
```

Or by hand:

```bash
dotnet restore [[ sln ]]
dotnet run --project [[ api_project ]]
```

The script sets the configuration the API needs; by hand you set it yourself, as below.

## Calling an endpoint

Every controller requires a bearer token; `/health` does not.

```bash
TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:[[ port ]]/auth/dev-token \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"subject":"local-run"}' \
  | sed -n 's/.*"access_token":"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p')

curl -s http://localhost:[[ port ]]/health
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" http://localhost:[[ port ]]/swagger/v1/swagger.json
```

`POST /auth/dev-token` exists only when `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development` **and**
`Auth__Mode=Development`, and it is compiled out of Release builds entirely. A deployed instance
validates Microsoft Entra ID tokens instead.

## Run the tests

```bash
dotnet test [[ sln ]]
```

The test project starts its own Postgres through Testcontainers, so Docker has to be running. The
same command runs in the Build step of ExiQO Migrate, so the result here should match what the
build recorded.

## The configuration this uses

| Key | Local value | What it means |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT` | `Development` | Turns on Swagger, CORS for `http://localhost:3000` and the development endpoints. |
| `Auth__Mode` | `Development` | Issues and accepts the development JWT above. Rejected in any other environment. |
| `Messaging__Mode` | `InMemory` | Replaces Azure Service Bus with an in-process bus, so no Azure account is needed. |
| `KeyVault__Uri` | empty | With a vault URI set, secrets come from Azure Key Vault under the workload's managed identity. |
[% for name, db in connection_strings %]
| `ConnectionStrings__[[ name ]]` | `Host=…;Database=[[ db ]];Username=app;Password=app` | The Postgres that replaces the TIBCO JDBC resource `[[ name ]]`. |
[% endfor %]

The Postgres credentials above are local-only and appear nowhere in the deployment: the Terragrunt
units under `infra/live/` provision a Postgres Flexible Server and a Key Vault, and the application
reads its secrets from the vault with its managed identity.

## What else is in this folder

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `[[ sln ]]` | The solution: `src/` holds the API, domain and infrastructure projects, `tests/` the generated tests. |
| `Dockerfile` | Multi-stage build producing `[[ api_dll ]]`. `CONFIGURATION=Debug` keeps the development endpoints; Release removes them. |
| `docker-compose.override.yml` | The local stack this script starts. Works on its own: `docker compose -f docker-compose.override.yml up --build`. |
| `infra/modules/` | Terraform modules, one per Azure resource. |
| `infra/live/<env>/` | Terragrunt units: one per module per environment, with the wiring between them. |
| `infra/bootstrap/tfstate.sh` | Creates the storage account the Terraform state lives in. |
| `.github/workflows/`, `azure-pipelines.yml` | The pipelines that build the image and apply the infrastructure. |

## If it does not start

| What you see | Why, and what to do |
| --- | --- |
| `docker is not on PATH` | Install Docker Desktop, or use the `--dotnet` path against your own Postgres. |
| `the Docker daemon is not running` | Start Docker Desktop and run the script again. |
| The script waits on `/health` and gives up | Read the container log: `docker compose -f docker-compose.override.yml logs api`. A failure on a connection string means Postgres was not reachable at the host the container uses. |
| `dotnet restore` fails | This machine needs access to the NuGet feed named in `nuget.config`. |
| Port [[ port ]] already in use | Something else is on it. Change the left-hand side of the `ports:` mapping in `docker-compose.override.yml`, or set `ASPNETCORE_URLS` for the `--dotnet` path. |
| A controller answers `401` | The bearer token is missing or expired. Get a new one from `POST /auth/dev-token` and use Swagger's **Authorize** dialog. |
